+
+    - an opener, an optional gap, an optional `/`, `!` or `?`, a name (a
+      letter, then letters, digits, `-`, `_`, `:`, `.`), zero or more
+      attributes (a name, optionally `=` and a quoted value or an unquoted
+      value with no space and no bracket), an optional `/`, then a closer
+      or the end of the text;
+    - a gap is any space or invisible character (control, format, combining
+      mark or variation selector). Only ASCII whitespace can be a
+      comparison gap: after one, no attributes are allowed, only the name,
+      an optional `/` and the closer or the end ("< question>" is a tag,
+      "a < b and c > d" is not). Any other gap, and a `/`, `!` or `?`,
+      always makes a tag;
+    - after `/`, `!` or `?` everything to the closer is the tag's body (as
+      an HTML reader takes it), and a comment `<!--` is dropped alone, its
+      text scanned on;
+    - an opener inside a span ends it. The span is a tag only when that
+      opener is itself a tag (then it goes first, and the span with it,
+      because removing the inner tag could leave a new one), or when the
+      span started with `/`, `!` or `?`. An opener followed, after a gap or
+      mark, by an opener that is a tag also goes.
+
+    The brackets, mark and closer go; the name and attributes stay. Every
+    other `<` and `>` stays as written: "c.3140-26A>G", "3849+10kbC->T",
+    "<5 years", "p<0.05", "a < b and c > d", "AUD<MC, pcor=.016) and (AUD>MC",
+    "Ca2+ >", "IL-6 > IL-10". A text with no tag-shaped span is returned
+    unchanged.
+
+    Why one pass of removals leaves no tag: a kept opener is followed in the
+    output by its own original text up to the point where its grammar
+    failed, and an opener right after it was judged not a tag; a removal
+    always inserts a letter after a space, never a closer or a bracket.
+    """
+    if "<" not in text and all(ord(c) < 0x80 for c in text):
+        return text
+    openers = [i for i, c in enumerate(text) if _angle_kind(c) == "open"]
